{"step_index":75,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-30T18:44:43Z","content":"Priyanshu, here's every M4 → M5 jump calculated from verified benchmarks. No rounding in your friend's favor.\n\n## Every M4 → M5 Percentage Gain (Base Chips, Verified)\n\n| Spec | M4 | M5 | Jump | 2x/3x? |\n|:---|:---|:---|:---|:---|\n| **Single-Core CPU** (Geekbench 6) | ~3,850 | ~4,285 | **+11%** | ❌ No |\n| **Multi-Core CPU** (Geekbench 6) | ~14,700 | ~17,850 | **+21%** | ❌ No |\n| **GPU Metal** (Geekbench 6) | ~57,500 | ~76,120 | **+32%** | ❌ No |\n| **Cinebench 2024 Single-Core** | ~175 | ~208 | **+19%** | ❌ No |\n| **Cinebench 2024 Multi-Core** | ~960 | ~1,275 | **+33%** | ❌ No |\n| **Memory Bandwidth** | 120 GB/s | 153 GB/s | **+28%** | ❌ No |\n| **P-Core Clock Speed** | 4.51 GHz | 4.61 GHz | **+2%** | ❌ No |\n| **E-Core Clock Speed** | 2.90 GHz | 3.00 GHz | **+3%** | ❌ No |\n| **Battery Life** | 18h / 15h | 18h / 15h | **0%** | ❌ Identical |\n| **Base RAM** | 16 GB | 16 GB | **0%** | ❌ Identical |\n| **GPU Core Count** | 10 | 10 | **0%** | ❌ Identical |\n| **Neural Engine (NPU) core count** | 16 | 16 | **0%** | ❌ Identical |\n| | | | | |\n| **AI/ML inference via MLX** (local LLMs, Whisper) | baseline | ~2.5x throughput | **+150%** | ✅ **YES — ~2.5x** |\n| **Apple's claim: \"Peak GPU AI compute\"** | baseline | \">4x\" | **+300%** | ✅ **Apple says 4x** |\n\n## Where Your Friend Is Right — and Where He's Wrong\n\n**Your friend is right about ONE thing:** AI/ML workload throughput. The M5's GPU Neural Accelerators (which don't exist on M4 at all) give roughly **2.5x real-world** throughput for local LLM inference, Whisper transcription, Stable Diffusion, and MLX-based tasks. Apple markets this as \"4x peak GPU AI compute\" but real benchmarks land at ~2.5x in sustained use.\n\n**Your friend is wrong if he thinks CPU, GPU, or general performance is 2x-3x.** It's not. It's:\n- CPU: **11-21%** faster\n- GPU: **32%** faster  \n- Memory: **28%** more bandwidth\n\nThese are solid generational gains, but nowhere close to 2x.\n\n## Where Does the 2.5x AI Jump Come From?\n\nThe M4 has **zero** Neural Accelerators in its GPU cores. Its AI compute relies entirely on the 16-core NPU (38 TOPS) and software-based GPU compute via Metal shaders.\n\nThe M5 added a **dedicated hardware tensor unit inside every GPU core**. So the M5 has two AI compute paths running simultaneously:\n1. The 16-core NPU (doing its thing)\n2. **10 GPU cores × 1 Neural Accelerator each** (doing matrix math in parallel)\n\nThe M4 only has path #1. The M5 has both. That's where the 2.5x comes from — it's not that the NPU got 2.5x faster, it's that the GPU joined the AI workload for the first time.\n\n## The Honest Bottom Line for Your Purchase Decision\n\nIf you're buying your friend's M5 Air 15-inch secondhand (3 months old), the question is really: **what's the price vs buying a new M4 Air 15-inch?**\n\n- For **daily computing** (browsing, coding, office, media): M4 and M5 feel identical. 11% single-core CPU gain is invisible.\n- For **local AI work**: M5 is genuinely 2.5x faster. This is real and significant.\n- For **GPU-heavy tasks** (video editing, games): M5 is ~32% faster. Noticeable but not life-changing.\n- **Wi-Fi 7 and Bluetooth 6** are on M5, not M4. Matters if you have a Wi-Fi 7 router.\n\nSo: if your friend is selling at a fair used price and you care about AI/ML at all, the M5 is worth it. If you don't care about local AI and the price difference vs a new M4 Air is significant — the M4 would serve you identically for everything else.","thinking":"The user wants to know the exact percentage jumps from M4 to M5 specifically. They want to know if there's really a 2x or 3x jump anywhere. Let me pull the precise numbers from my research and give a clean, honest answer.\n\nLet me calculate the M4 → M5 percentage gains from the verified data I have:\n\n1. **Geekbench 6 Single-Core**: M4 ~3,850 → M5 ~4,285 = +11.3%\n2. **Geekbench 6 Multi-Core**: M4 ~14,700 → M5 ~17,850 = +21.4%\n3. **GPU Metal**: +32.4%, **Cinebench SC**: +18.9%, **Cinebench MC**: +32.8%\n4. **Neural Engine**: unchanged at 38 TOPS but Apple claims >4x GPU AI compute\n5. **Memory Bandwidth**: +27.5%, **P-Core Clock**: +2.2%, **E-Core Clock**: +3.4%\n\nSo the real gains cluster around 10-33% depending on workload, with GPU-bound and multi-threaded tasks seeing the biggest jumps, while raw clock speeds barely moved — the improvements are coming from architecture and core count, not frequency.\n\nThe 2x-3x claim only holds for AI/ML workloads using the Neural Accelerators specifically — real-world MLX inference benchmarks show around 2.5x throughput, but that's not representative of general performance. I need to be clear that outside of AI tasks, we're looking at 11-33% gains, not the 2-3x the friend is claiming.\n\n"}
